          "text": "On 1 May 2014, Turkish authorities barred May Day rallies in Istanbul’s Taksim Square. Police sealed off access, restricted public transport, and used tear gas and water cannon against crowds attempting to approach; clashes, injuries, and detentions followed. The government defended the restrictions on security and public-order grounds and offered alternative venues.\n\nThe decision was politically charged because Taksim is central to Turkey’s labour movement, particularly the memory of the deadly 1977 May Day gathering. It also carried the legacy of the 2013 Gezi Park protests. Excluding demonstrators therefore affected not only where they could assemble, but their ability to communicate a historically significant political message. Critics viewed the ban as further evidence of Prime Minister Recep Tayyip Erdoğan’s intolerance of dissent.\n\nAuthorities had a legitimate duty to protect safety, and violence by individual protesters could warrant targeted intervention. Nevertheless, restrictions on peaceful assembly should be necessary, proportionate, and nondiscriminatory. A blanket exclusion, enforced coercively, was difficult to justify without demonstrating why less restrictive arrangements—such as negotiated access and targeted policing—would be inadequate. Overall, the action disproportionately constrained political expression and labour organizing, undermining democratic participation and risking deeper public mistrust rather than constructively managing a foreseeable gathering."
